/Murthy_v._Missouri. *Maps to 6B.5 (opaque rather than transparent-correction).*\n\n29. **Hurricane Idalia and Maui wildfire response received plaudits but Maui disaster also exposed underlying failures in emergency-management infrastructure (Maui Police Department preliminary after-action report identified 32 recommendations including equipment updates and inter-agency communication gaps — none of which were addressed in formal accountability review by federal government in 2023).** (FEMA USFA preliminary report, Feb 8, 2024; Wikipedia \"2023 Maui wildfires\") — https://www.usfa.fema.gov/blog/preliminary-after-action-report-2023-maui-wildfire/ ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2023_Maui_wildfires. *Maps to 6B.5 (after-action but no formal federal accountability).*\n\n30. **COVID-era Title 42 public-health expulsion authority ended May 11, 2023 — the transition to standard Title 8 processing created a measurable back-log without a fully corrected measurement system in place.** (general — DHS/Congressional Research Service) — *Maps to 6B.5 (operational measurement decline).*\n\n## Mixed Evidence\n\n31. **Bipartisan Safer Communities Act implementation in 2023 was data-driven (enhanced NICS background checks, ATF rule on ghost-gun definitions) — implementation timelines and metrics varied across jurisdictions.** (Wikipedia, \"Presidency of Joe Biden\"; Everytown 2023) — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidency_of_Joe_Biden ; https://www.everytown.org/year-in-review-2023/. *Maps to 6B.5 (partial; mixed).*\n\n32. **Pennsylvania's use of American Rescue Plan CVI funds was documented as an evidence-based case study in violence-reduction — and Biden cited it in establishing the federal Office — but the federal evaluation framework for that approach was still being built in 2023.** (Cross-reference Wikipedia \"Crime in the United States\"; The Trace; Joyce Foundation) — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crime_in_the_United_States ; https://www.thetrace.org/2024/10/biden-administration-gun-violence-prevention/. *Maps to 6B.5 (evidence-informed but not fully measured at the federal level).*\n\n33. **Iran-Saudi rapprochement (March 2023) and Yemen peace negotiations (April 2023) were supported diplomatically by the U.S.; measurable ceasefire / reduction-in-violence data was collected in 2023 but the lens requires sustained monitoring (the Houthi Red Sea attacks starting October 19, 2023 disrupted progress).** (Wikipedia, \"Yemeni civil war\") — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yemeni_civil_war_(2014%E2%80%93present). *Maps to 6B.5 mixed.*\n\n## Net Assessment\n\n**On evidence, measurement, and transparent correction in service delivery in 2023, the Biden record is mixed-to-favorable with clear evidence of progress on the institutional infrastructure but mixed results on correction and outcomes:**\n\n**Strongly favorable — institutional evidence infrastructure:**\n- Continued implementation of the bipartisan Evidence Act (2018) — Chief Evaluation Officers, agency Evidence-Building Plans, Open Data Plans, statistical-agency independence, Chief Data Officers — all in operation across the executive branch.\n- External expert engagement maintained in arms control, AI safety (May 30 Statement; AI Safety and Security Board), CBRN governance, gun violence prevention, biosafety, and the National Cybersecurity Strategy.\n- Public Federal-Register publication of major EOs (Fiscal Responsibility Act, AI EO 14110, AUKUS-optimal-pathway, National Cybersecurity Strategy) with explicit fact-sheet transparency.\n\n**Favorable — domain-specific evidence use:**\n- White House Office of Gun Violence Prevention (Sep 21, 2023) integrated CDC public-health framing, CVI program evidence (murder-rate –12% in 90+ cities H1 2023), and external progress measurement. Year-one report (Sep 2024) documented measurable progress.\n- Pre-existing Hur-investigation precedent showed independent accountability measurement.\n- Federal Reserve's data-driven rate cycle in 2023.\n- Inflation Reduction Act drug-price negotiation (Aug 2023 first 10 drugs) selected based on Medicare Part D cost and utilization data.\n- CHIPS Act NSF TIP directorate launch on evidence base.\n\n**Unfavorable — correction absent or limited:**\n- CDC Dickey Amendment constraint not removed.\n- Medicaid unwinding: ~12.8M disenrolled with limited corrective protective measures.\n- Food insecurity rose to decade-high 13.5% despite continued SNAP/P-EBT; SNAP emergency-allotment unwind was empirically known to risk food security but proceeded.\n- Sentinel ICBM cost overrun publicly acknowledged but program continued without restructuring — partial transparent acknowledgment without structural correction.\n- Murthy v. Missouri documented opaque platform-pressure criteria.\n- Maui disaster management showed after-action-review recommendations issued but no formal federal corrective accountability in 2023.\n\n**Honest read:** A lens-scoped comparison yields **net-mixed-to-favorable** on institutional evidence infrastructure and domain-specific measurement; **net-unfavorable** on selective corrective action where empirical evidence warned of risks (food insecurity, Medicaid unwinding) but corrective policy did not fully follow; **mixed** on the broader effectiveness of measurement informing policy outcomes. The Biden administration's 2023 record was strong on *publishing* evidence-based plans (EOs, Office of Gun Violence Prevention, NCS, NAS framework) but **weak on systematically *correcting* programs whose data showed underperformance** — the lens's \"transparent correction\" component is the weakest link.\n\n---\n\n## Search-Gap Note\n\nFour targeted searches and four focused fetches were performed in this session. The following are noted as residual gaps:\n- Direct ASPE 2023 evaluation officer directory / Chief Data Officer directory was not pulled\n- Direct 2023 GAO high-risk list mentions were not pulled\n- Direct 2023 IG semiannual report to Congress specific findings were 